        "The underlying rationale common across religions: interest reinforces wealth concentration, expands risk-free relative to risk-related capital, and drives business failure, unemployment and inequality leading to social/economic instability.",
        "The legal 'usury vs interest' distinction (usury = above a legal maximum, prohibited; interest = below it, permitted) dates specifically to a 1545 English legal division by the King and Church, not to any principled Shari'ah-type distinction.",
        "The theoretical justification for charging interest was launched by Calvin and Molinaeus in the mid-16th century, gaining momentum with expanding trade and becoming entrenched with the 18th-century Industrial Revolution's demand for investment capital.",
        "Both the Bible and the Qur'an can be read as forbidding usury/interest outright — the EH.Net Encyclopedia notes ongoing moral debate in the West about whether any interest-charging is permissible, despite the modern legal narrowing of 'usury' to only excessive rates."
