      overview: "The author defines Maisir and Qimar, gives their Qur'anic basis, explains how gambling is a form of Gharar, and applies the prohibition to modern lotteries, prize bonds, conventional insurance, and price-difference-settled futures/options.",
      simpleExplanation: "Maisir/Qimar means gambling — getting wealth by pure chance without earning it through work, risk-bearing, or giving something of equal value in return, where one party's gain is exactly another's loss. The Qur'an bans it directly. Modern examples include lotteries, casino-style prize bonds where a lucky few get huge prizes funded by everyone else's forgone interest, conventional insurance (which combines interest and gambling elements), and futures/options contracts settled purely by price differences with no real intention of delivering the underlying asset. Not every drawing of lots is prohibited though — if nobody has a pre-existing claim and no one is deprived of what they already had, drawing lots to fairly allocate something can be legitimate.",
      academicExplanation: "Maisir and Qimar are used near-interchangeably in Arabic; Qimar is described by jurists as a specific/important subset of Maisir. Maisir (from 'Yusr', ease) means acquiring something valuable with ease, without paying an equivalent compensation ('Iwad), without working for it, or without undertaking any liability against it, via a game of chance; Qimar means receiving money/benefit/usufruct at others' cost, via chance-based entitlement. The Qur'an uses 'Maisir' for the prohibition (verses 2:219, 5:90-91); Hadith literature generally discusses it as 'Qimar'. Key verses: 5:90 (intoxicants and gambling are 'abominable actions of Satan' to be abstained from); 5:91 (Satan uses intoxicants/gambling to excite enmity/hatred and to hinder remembrance of Allah/prayer); 4:219 (wine and gambling contain 'great sin and some benefits... but the sin is greater than the benefits'). Gambling is characterized as a form of Gharar, since the gambler is ignorant of the gamble's result. Pakistan's Federal Shariat Court (FSC) rulings are cited: a lottery inducing participation via an uncertain/unknown chance-based event, or distributing disproportionate prizes by drawing lots, is repugnant to Shari'ah; a scheme where investors' principal is safe/intact but prizes are funded from interest generated on pooled capital is also repugnant, due to Qimar. The author gives a test distinguishing valid from invalid 'lottery'-type mechanisms: in valid cases, no participant has any personal/vested right at stake and no one is deprived of something they already possessed or contributed — drawing lots is then simply a neutral, fair allocation method among equals (e.g. Prophet Younus's case, or a grantor with equal-footing candidates choosing by lot); similarly, promotional giveaways where a purchaser knowingly buys a known product at a known price, with an extra incentive item given (no drawing/chance involved), are permissible, since there's no element of chance in what was actually purchased. However, where the mechanism causes loss to some participants for others to gain (disproportionate prizes via drawing of lots, chance-based inducements via tickets/coupons/tabs), it is prohibited — the author notes such schemes often combine Riba, Gharar, Jahl and Qimar simultaneously, since the pooled proceeds function as debt and the 'prize' is really a redistributed portion of predetermined interest payments from the bank (borrower) to lenders, while exaggerated publicity (implying near-certain winning odds when true odds are one in hundreds of thousands) constitutes additional deception. Regarding financial institutions specifically: prize bond schemes (investors' principal safe, but prizes funded from interest on pooled capital, distributed by chance to a few at others' expense) are ruled repugnant due to both Riba and Maisir; schemes giving most participants a meagre/no return while diverting the difference to a few large 'prizes' cause loss to the majority for the benefit of a few, diverting resources from real economic activity into speculation. The chapter also flags related modern applications: conventional insurance is deemed non-Shari'ah-compliant due to both Riba and Maisir involvement; government/corporate resource mobilization via lottery/draws is prohibited as gambling; futures and options contracts settled purely through price differences (without genuine delivery intent) fall under the gambling prohibition.",
      examExplanation: "Maisir = acquiring wealth easily/by chance without equivalent compensation, work, or liability; Qimar = a subset of Maisir, receiving money/benefit at another's cost via chance. Qur'anic basis: 2:219, 5:90-91 (gambling grouped with intoxicants as 'abominable Satanic actions'). Gambling is a form of Gharar (the gambler doesn't know the outcome). Test for valid vs invalid lot-drawing: valid if no participant has a pre-existing vested right and no one is deprived of something they already had/contributed (neutral fair allocation among equals); invalid if it causes loss to some for others' disproportionate gain (typical prize-bond/lottery structure). Modern applications ruled prohibited: government/corporate lotteries and draws; bank prize-bond schemes (funded from pooled-capital interest, combining Riba + Maisir); conventional insurance (Riba + Maisir); futures/options settled purely by price differences without genuine delivery intent.",
