        "'Aqilah (tribal shared-responsibility for calamity/blood-money) and Dhaman Khatr al-Tariq (pooled indemnification of trade-route losses) are pre-Islamic customs validated by Islam, and are identified as the direct historical foundation of the modern Takaful institution.",
        "Fair prices must emerge from genuinely free market forces; Ghaban-e-Fahish (charging an inflated price while implying it is the market rate) is prohibited, and selling below cost out of misplaced philanthropy is also discouraged since it distorts future supply (Caliph Umar precedent).",
        "The OIC Fiqh Academy (1988) ruled there is no fixed restriction on a trader's profit percentage (left to the merchant, market and goods, subject to Shari'ah ethics), and that government should not fix prices except to remove clearly identified market distortions/fraud.",
        "Freedom from Dharar protects third parties from harm caused by a contract between two other consenting parties; Shuf'ah (a partner's pre-emptive right in joint ownership) illustrates this, and the author suggests it could analogously protect minority shareholders from harmful decisions by controlling shareholders."
